WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
I as a historian know, there is

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.660
very little we can say about the next

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:08.789
100 years, let alone 10,000 years, for sure.

00:00:08.789 --> 00:00:10.920
On the other hand we can say a lot

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.530
about the last 10,000 years. And actually it's

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.479
quite stunning how much more we know

00:00:15.479 --> 00:00:17.370
now than we did a hundred years ago because

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.680
of the information memory technologies

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.260
that we're inventing now to mine material

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.510
evidence for information that we never

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.039
would have thought possible. Including

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:29.880
DNA forensic analysis. So I want

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.940
to focus tonight on what we can learn

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.360
about the future by looking at the past.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.580
So I want to say that if you're really

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.120
interested in the next 10,000 years

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.530
you have no better evidence to

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.200
look to than the past 10,000 years

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.000
and that's what I want to talk about tonight.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.370
Now the story actually begins

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.020
a lot earlier than "the long now." It begins

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:57.020
in the long ago. This actually dates

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.300
from about 40,000 years ago, we think.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.540
I'll talk a little bit more about it.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.140
But I think that memory technologies

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.850
and in fact human species really took

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:09.180
a real pivotal turn. That moment sort of

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.650
in the Garden of Eden or at least it was a

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.720
Garden of Eden moment when we realized

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.659
that we're going to die. As far as we

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:17.580
know there is no other species

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.280
that understands that they are going to die.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.540
Not just that their own partners

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.189
or something dies, their children dies...

00:01:23.189 --> 00:01:25.650
But that we anticipate that we're going to

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.080
die. We know that. And I think that it

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.000
changes everything for the way humans

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.250
think about time. Now I want to talk

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.560
a little bit more about that but this is

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.170
early evidence from long ago, about

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.140
40,000 years ago, that human beings

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.150
from the very beginnings became obsessed

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.119
with cheating death, in fact. And i look at

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.020
this technology of paint on cave walls

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.440
as the earliest concrete evidence of

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.360
cheating death by in fact extrapolating

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.700
the contents of our mental life our

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.769
imagination of you know our sort of

00:01:58.769 --> 00:02:01.650
spiritual life. And putting it in a safe

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.170
place. Actually we're outsourcing it to

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.750
durable physical objects. And you can see

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.280
it's very familiar. We don't know

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.950
what these people are telling us. But

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.870
we know that they're human. And we

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.000
know that because they actually signed their

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.130
own piece of art. I think of this as the

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.560
earliest kind of... it's a Paleolithic

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.580
selfie, in a way. You know what's so

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.710
interesting is that as a species we are

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.140
not the strongest. We are not the fastest.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.480
And we are not even the brainiest. I hate

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.190
to say it. But we actually don't have the

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.379
biggest brains. And we certainly don't

00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:36.420
live the longest. The one thing that we

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.670
have is this amazing thing that we have

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.040
collectively created which is our

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.960
collective memory. A kind of collective

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.000
brain which is the thing that we keep

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.640
alive over generations. Which makes us

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.770
able to accumulate knowledge over time.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.200
And actually we are now at this juncture

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.420
on the planet when we are not only the

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.429
dominant species on the planet, but

00:02:56.429 --> 00:02:57.960
through our collective memory and

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.520
collective brain we are dangerously

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.770
dominant over the planet. That's another

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:02.849
reason why I think this is a really

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.640
critical time for us. You know

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.940
the sense that we have the certainty that we

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.040
are separate from other people that we're

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.100
going to die and this collective memory

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.060
that we have, have endowed us with

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.480
a couple of unique qualities from other species.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.740
We have a certain temporal depth

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.990
perception which is very deep. Very

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.820
deep into the future and very deep into the past.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.460
And through this temporal depth perception

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.200
we actually can engage in mental time travel.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.840
We can deduce what happened

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.460
in the past, and we can project

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.480
that into the future.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.860
We can engage in conjectural thinking.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.680
And through language in fact, we can engage

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.629
in what I think of as synchronized

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:45.990
mental time travel. No better example is

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:49.020
that crew that 18 or 20 years ago

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.179
started to put together the New Horizons

00:03:51.179 --> 00:03:54.300
mission to Pluto. And that team

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.819
and people cycled in and out of that team

00:03:56.819 --> 00:03:59.939
at the JPL. That team was engaged in mental

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:03.270
time travel synchronized for an astounding

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.069
period of time and how they ended up

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:07.439
actually hitting their target after that.

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:09.390
Especially after the appropriations

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.040
scares they received along the way

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.620
from our friends in Congress. It's really

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.140
a testimony to how collective action and

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.020
collective memory really is

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:19.360
a very powerful thing.

